Goal: write the tests for the checkpoint BEFORE the code. A checkpoint saves one tick: the new messages, the operations they start, and the new state. The store has no transactions, so the order of writes is the design.

Do: create internal/agentjob/checkpoint.go with only this:
type Checkpoint struct { Messages []LoopMessage; Ops []LoopOp; State State }
// failAfter is a test hook: Checkpoint returns errInjected right after finishing step n (1 = messages appended, 2 = ops written). 0 = never.
var failAfter int
var errInjected = errors.New("injected failure")
func (s *Store) Checkpoint(id string, cp Checkpoint) error { return errors.New("not implemented") }
func (s *Store) RecoverLoop(id string) error { return errors.New("not implemented") }

Create internal/agentjob/checkpoint_test.go. Start EVERY test with: t.Skip("enabled by card m1-2e"). Tests:
- TestCheckpointHappy: a job in status running; Checkpoint with one assistant message whose Content is {"tool_calls":[{"id":"call_1","name":"shell","arguments":{"cmd":"ls"}}]}, one LoopOp{ID:"op1", ToolCallID:"call_1", Tool:"shell", Status:OpPending}, and State.Status "waiting" with WaitReason WaitOperation. After it: Messages has 1 entry, Ops has 1, Get shows waiting.
- TestCheckpointCrashAfterMessages: set failAfter=1; Checkpoint returns errInjected; then RecoverLoop(id); expect: Messages has 1 entry, Ops has 1 op with ID "call_1" (rebuilt from the tool call, Status pending, Tool "shell"), state is waiting with WaitReason operation.
- TestCheckpointCrashAfterOps: failAfter=2; then RecoverLoop; expect the same end result as the happy case.
- TestRecoverLoopNoToolCalls: last message is an assistant message with no tool_calls and state still says running; RecoverLoop sets status to ready (the tick must be decided again by the loop).
- TestRecoverLoopIdempotent: calling RecoverLoop twice changes nothing the second time.
Reset failAfter to 0 with t.Cleanup in each test.

